= Cognition =

== Overview ==
'''Cognition''' is the collection of processes and capacities involved in acquiring, representing, transforming, using, and regulating information or knowledge.

Cognitive science studies processes such as [[Perception]], [[Attention]], [[Memory]], [[Learning]], [[Reasoning]], language, decision making, and [[Metacognition]].

Cognition is not one single mechanism. It is a family of interacting processes studied across psychology, neuroscience, linguistics, philosophy, computer science, anthropology, and related fields.

== Core processes ==
Commonly studied cognitive functions include perceiving and identifying information; selecting what to process; encoding and retrieving memories; learning regularities and skills; forming concepts and representations; reasoning and solving problems; planning and deciding; understanding language; and monitoring and regulating one's own thinking.

These divisions are useful analytical categories, not guaranteed natural modules.

== Cognition and consciousness ==
Cognition is not identical to consciousness.

Some cognitive processing is consciously accessible, while other processing can influence behavior without being reportable.

Likewise, conscious experience includes affective and bodily dimensions that should not simply be reduced to cognition by definition.

== Cognition and intelligence ==
Intelligence concerns capacities for effective reasoning, learning, adaptation, or problem solving under particular theories and measurements.

Cognition is broader. A cognitive process can be simple, automatic, biased, or domain-specific without being a measure of intelligence.

== Individual and distributed cognition ==
Traditional cognitive science often studies processes within individuals.

[[Distributed cognition]] expands the unit of analysis to coordinated systems containing people, tools, artifacts, and representations.

[[Embodied cognition]] emphasizes the role of bodily systems, sensorimotor capacities, and environmental interaction.

These approaches revise where cognitive explanation is located; they do not eliminate internal cognitive processes.

== Epistemic notes ==
Cognition is an established scientific subject, but its architecture is contested. Disputes concern modularity, representation, embodiment, consciousness, neural realization, social distribution, and the explanatory role of computation.

